          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            This page refers to a version of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haag-Kastler+axioms"} Haag-Kastler axioms
            for
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
            %em the set
            of Haag-Kastler axioms, but one specific choice. The purpose is to state and prove some of the classical results of the theory in a mathematically precise and model-independent way. Experts may find the chosen axioms to be stronger than necessary, this is deliberately so to ease the exposition.
          %p It is possible to construct examples that fulfill the axioms, to show that they are not empty, but we will not engage in this task here, at least not now. Note however that up to now there was no success in the task to construct systems in 4 dimensions with interactions, which has led to some doubts about the usefulness of this approach in the physics community: It has yet to be shown if the approach does or does not capture the essential features that makes possible the tremendous success of the standard model of particle physics.
          %h2#abstract Abstract
          %p We first collect some terms that are needed to then formulate the axioms of the vacuum representation. A few simple consequences are cited and some links to further concepts are provided.
          %h3#notation Notation
          %h4#the_poincar_group The Poincaré Group
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+cover"} universal cover
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi SL
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⋉
                  %msup
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} R
                    %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SL(2, \C) \ltimes \R^4
            of the restricted
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincar%C3%A9+group"} Poincaré group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi 𝒫
                    %mo +
                    %mo †
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}^{\dagger}_+
            will be denoted by
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒫
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+group"} abelian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subgroup"} subgroup
            of all translations by
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}
          %h4#the_minkowski_spacetime The Minkowski Spacetime
          %p
            We consider 4-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℳ𝒾𝓃
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{Min}
            only, i.e.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℳ𝒾𝓃
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{Min}
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+space"} vector space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} R
                    %mn 4
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} R
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} R
                    %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \R^{4} = \R \times \R^{3}
            equipped with the scalar product
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo &gt;
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 0
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mn 0
                  %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo ,
                  %mover
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lt x, y \gt := x_0 y_0 - (\vec x, \vec y)
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ( \cdot , \cdot  )
            being the Euclidean scalar product on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} R
                      %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \R^{3}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℳ𝒾𝓃
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{Min}
            will be denoted by
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒪
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒪
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}
            form an index set
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒥
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{J}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} partially ordered
            by inclusion.
          %p
            If two sets are spacelike separated, this will be denoted by
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ⊥
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}_1 \perp \mathcal{O}_2
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒪
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}
            will be denoted by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒪
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}'
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mo ⊥
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}^{\perp}
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+index+set"} causal index set
          %p
            We denote the open forward (light)cone at x by
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mo +
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_+(x)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_-(x)
            is the open backward cone at
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x=0
            we simply write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_+
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mo −
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_-
            %strong double cone
            or
            %strong diamond
            is an intersection of an open forward cone and an open backward cone that is nonempty and will be denoted by
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒦
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{K}
          %p
            An important class of unbounded regions are the
            = succeed ":" do
              %em wedges
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi W
                    %mi R
                  %mo ≔
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi x
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 3
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mn 4
                  %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                  %mo &gt;
                  %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  W_R \coloneqq \{ x = (t, x_1, x_2, x_3) \in \mathbb{R}^4 \mid x_1 \gt \shortmid t \shortmid \}
          %p The set of wedges is then defined to be
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒲
                  %mo ≔
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi λ
                  %msub
                    %mi W
                    %mi R
                  %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                  %mi λ
                  %mo ∈
                  %msubsup
                    %mi 𝒫
                    %mo +
                    %mo †
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathcal{W} \coloneqq \{\lambda W_R \mid \lambda \in \mathcal{P}^{\dagger}_+ \}
          %p While the definition of the right wedge depends on the chosen initial frame, the definition of the set of wedges does not.
          %h4#operator_algebras Operator Algebras
          %p
            For background see at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operator+algebra"} operator algebra
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/von+Neumann+algebra"} von Neumann algebra
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/von+Neumann+algebra"} Von Neumann algebras
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℳ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}
            will always be concrete
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operator+algebras"} operator algebras
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/module"} acting
            on a given
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℋ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{H}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutant"} commutant
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℳ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}
            will be denoted by
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℳ
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}'
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℳ
                      %mo +
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}^+
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℳ
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}_1
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℳ
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}_2
            will be denoted by:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℳ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ∨
                  %msub
                    %mi ℳ
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ≔
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi ℳ
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ∪
                    %msub
                      %mi ℳ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ″
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathcal{M}_1 \vee \mathcal{M}_2 \coloneqq  {(\mathcal{M}_1 \cup \mathcal{M}_2)}''
          %p
            As in this formula we will make frequently use of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicommutant+theorem"} bicommutant theorem
            to denote by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℳ
                  %mo ″
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}''
            the weak resp. strong closure resp. the generated von Neumann algebra of a give set of bounded operators.
          %p
            An
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automorphism"} automorphism
            of an algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                  %mi ℳ
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℳ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha \mathcal{M} \rightarrow \mathcal{M}
            is called an
            %strong inner
            automorphism if there is an invertible element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi u
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℳ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u \in \mathcal{M}
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
            is given by conjugation with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi u
                  %mo :
                  %mi α
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi m
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi u
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %mi m
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %msup
                    %mi u
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %mo ∀
                  %mi m
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℳ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u: \alpha(m) = u \quad m \quad u^{-1} \qquad \forall m\in \mathcal{M}
            (note that our convention here differs from that used by
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inner_automorphism"} Wikipedia
          %h4#group_representations Group Representations
          %p
            In this paragraph we will collect some links and remarks about unitary
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation"} representation
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+group"} topological group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒢
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}
            will be a topological group,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℋ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{H}
            a (complex) Hilbert space and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒰
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}
            an unitary representation of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒢
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}
            in the algebra of bounded operators of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℋ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{H}
          %p
            Definition (
            %strong> analytical vector
            ): Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒢
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}
            be a n-dimensional real Lie group. Fix a
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℋ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \in \mathcal{H}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} R
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \R^n
            and a parametrization
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo :
                  %mi B
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝒢
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi: B \to \mathcal{G}
            of a neighbourhood of 1 in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒢
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒰
                    %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}_f
            by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒰
                    %mi f
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi B
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℋ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathcal{U}_f \colon B \to \mathcal{H}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi 𝒰
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  x \mapsto \mathcal{U}\big(\phi(x)\big) f
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒰
                    %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}_f
            has an extension to an analytic function on a neighbourhood of 0 in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} C
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \C^n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is called an analytic vector (for
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒰
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}
          %p
            See
            %a{:href => "http://planetmath.org/encyclopedia/BanachSpaceValuedAnalyticFunctions.html"} planetmath
            for the definition of Banach space valued analytic functions.
          %h3#definition_of_vacuum_representations Definition of Vacuum Representations
          %p
            A net of von Neumann algebras
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℳ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒪
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}(\mathcal{O})
            on a common Hilbert space
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℋ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathcal{H})
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒪
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒥
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O} \in \mathcal{J}
            %strong vacuum respresentation
            (on the 4-dimensional Minkowski spacetime) if it satisfies the following axioms:
          #isotony.num_theorem
            %ul
              %li
                (V1)
                = succeed ":" do
                  %strong isotony
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒪
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ⊂
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒪
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}_1 \subset \mathcal{O}_2
                implies
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℳ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒪
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊆
                      %mi ℳ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒪
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}(\mathcal{O}_1) \subseteq \mathcal{M}(\mathcal{O}_2)
          #additivity.num_theorem
            %ul
              %li
                (V2)
                = succeed ":" do
                  %strong additivity
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋃
                        %mi j
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒪
                        %mi j
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O} = \bigcup_j \mathcal{O}_j
                implies
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℳ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋃
                        %mi j
                      %mi ℳ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒪
                        %mi j
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ″
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}(\mathcal{O}) = ( \bigcup_j \mathcal{M}(\mathcal{O}_j) )''
          #locality.num_theorem
            %ul
              %li
                (V3)
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong locality
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+net"} local net
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒪
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ⊥
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒪
                        %mn 2
                      %mi implies
                      %mi ℳ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒪
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊆
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ℳ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒪
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ′
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}_1 \perp \mathcal{O}_2 implies \mathcal{M}(\mathcal{O}_1) \subseteq  (\mathcal{M}(\mathcal{O}_2))'
          #covariance.num_theorem
            %ul
              %li
                (V4)
                = succeed ":" do
                  %strong covariance
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒰
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi 𝒫
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}(\mathcal{P})
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒫
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}
                such that for each
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi 𝒫
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g \in \mathcal{P}
                the following holds:
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℳ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi g
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi 𝒰
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi ℳ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi 𝒰
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi g
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}(g\mathcal{O}) = \mathcal{U}(g) \mathcal{M}(\mathcal{O}) \mathcal{U}(g)^{-1}
          #spectrum_condition.num_theorem
            %ul
              %li
                (V5)
                = succeed ":" do
                  %strong spectrum condition
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi spec
                      %mi 𝒰
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi 𝒯
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊆
                      %mi clo
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒱
                        %mo +
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} spec\mathcal{U}(\mathcal{T}) \subseteq clo \mathcal{V}_+
          %p
            %em Remark
            (mathematical viewpoint):
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}
            is the abelian subgroup of translations, and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒱
                    %mo +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{V}_+
            is the (open) forward cone at 0, see above. For the definiton of the spectrum of the representation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒰
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒯
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}(\mathcal{T})
            see
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+measure"} spectral measure
          #existence_of_vacuum_vector.num_theorem
            %ul
              %li
                (V6)
                = succeed ":" do
                  %strong existence of a vacuum vector
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Ω
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi ℋ
                        %mo ,
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                        %mi Ω
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                        %mo =
                        %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega \in \mathcal{H}, \|\Omega\| = 1
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                      %msub
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋃
                        %mrow
                          %mi 𝒪
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi 𝒥
                      %mi ℳ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                      %mi Ω
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigl( \bigcup_{\mathcal{O}\in\mathcal{J}}\mathcal{M}(\mathcal{O}) \bigr) \Omega
                is dense in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℋ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{H}
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒰
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi Ω
                      %mo =
                      %mi Ω
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}(g)\Omega = \Omega
                for all
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi 𝒫
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g \in \mathcal{P}
          .un_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              %strong (choice of axioms)
              The
              %em uniqueness
              is sometimes part of the axioms, but not here. Instead we will cite theorems that will specify necessary and sufficient conditions to ensure that there is a
              %em unique
              vacuum vector.
          %h3#additional_notations_and_notions_of_vacuum_representations Additional Notations and Notions of Vacuum Representations
          %p
            A short hand notation for vacuum representations will be
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℳ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒥
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}(\mathcal{J})
            in the following.
          %p
            The algebras
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℳ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒪
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}(\mathcal{O})
            are sometimes called
            = succeed "." do
              %strong local algebras
          %p
            The
            = succeed "algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^*-
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒜
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi clo
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋃
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi 𝒥
                  %mi ℳ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒪
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A} := clo_{\| \cdot \|} \bigl( \bigcup_{\mathcal{O}\in\mathcal{J}}\mathcal{M}(\mathcal{O}) \bigr)
            is called
            = succeed "," do
              %strong quasilocal algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
            is called the
            %strong global algebra
            and denoted by
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℛ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{R}
          %p
            A vacuum representation is called
            %strong irreducible
            if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℛ
                  %mo =
                  %mi ℒ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℋ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{R} = \mathcal{L}(\mathcal{H})
            (the global algebra is the whole algebra of all bounded linear operators on the given Hilbert space), it is called
            %strong factorial
            if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℛ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{R}
            is a factor.
          %ul
            %li
              Wikipedia on
              %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Von_Neumann_algebra#Factors"} factors
              of von Neumann algebras.
          %p
            The subspace of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℋ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{H}
            that is invariant under the action of the translation group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}
            is not trivial due to the axiom
            = succeed "." do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#existence_of_vacuum_vector"}
            %strong unique vacuum vector
            (the space is then necessarily the subspace
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} C
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \C\Omega
          %h3#first_consequences_and_the_reehschlieder_theorem first consequences and the Reeh-Schlieder theorem
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %strong Definition (algebras of unbounded open set)
                To any unbounded open set
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒰
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}
                we associate a von Neumann algebra by
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℳ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≔
                      %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋃
                        %mrow
                          %mi 𝒪
                          %mo ⊂
                          %mi 𝒰
                      %mi ℳ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                      %mo ″
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\mathcal{M}(U) \coloneqq \bigl( \bigcup_{\mathcal{O} \subset \mathcal{U}} \mathcal{M}(\mathcal{O}) \bigr)''
            %li
              %p
                %strong Theorem (weak additivity)
                Let
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi 𝒥
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O} \in \mathcal{J}
                be arbitrary, then we have
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋃
                        %mrow
                          %mi a
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi 𝒯
                      %mi ℳ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                      %mo ″
                      %mo =
                      %mi ℛ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\bigl( \bigcup_{a \in \mathcal{T}} \mathcal{M}(a \mathcal{O}) \bigr)'' = \mathcal{R}
          %p
            %em Remark
            (choice of axioms): Weak additivity of our nets are a direct consequence of our axioms, but this is often enough not so in the literature: Depending on the choice of axioms, it is either stated as an axiom or an auxiliary property of a net that is called for when needed.
          %p
            %em> Proof
            \: Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒪
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}_0
            be given (arbitrary but fixed). Let us first note that thanks to isotony and additivity we can restrict the index set of the union in the definition of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℛ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{R}
            from open bounded sets to diamonds, since every bounded set is contained in a diamond:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋃
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi 𝒥
                  %mi ℳ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒪
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %mo ″
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋃
                    %mi 𝒦
                  %mi ℳ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒦
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %mo ″
                  %mo =
                  %mi ℛ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\bigl( \bigcup_{\mathcal{O} \in \mathcal{J}} \mathcal{M}(\mathcal{O}) \bigr)'' = \bigl( \bigcup_{\mathcal{K}} \mathcal{M}(\mathcal{K}) \bigr)'' = \mathcal{R}
          %p
            Given an arbitrary diamond
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒦
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{K}
            we have
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒦
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋃
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi 𝒦
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒪
                    %mn 0
                  %mo +
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{K} \subset \bigl( \bigcup_{a \in \mathcal{K}}(\mathcal{O}_0 + a) \bigr)
            while the latter is a bounded open subset
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒥
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \in \mathcal{J}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋃
                    %mi 𝒦
                  %mi ℳ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒦
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋃
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒪
                        %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi 𝒯
                  %mi ℳ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒪
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\bigcup_{\mathcal{K}} \mathcal{M}(\mathcal{K}) = \bigcup_{a\mathcal{O}_0, a \in {\mathcal{T}}} \mathcal{M}(a\mathcal{O}_0)
          %p which implies that the generated von Neumann algebras coincide, too, of course.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %strong> Theorem (Borchers, translations are inner)
                \: The representatives of the translations are elements of the global algebra
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℛ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{R}
                = succeed ":" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℛ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{R}
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒰
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi 𝒯
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ⊆
                        %mi ℛ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}(\mathcal{T}) \subseteq \mathcal{R}
            %li
              %p
                %strong> Theorem (uniqueness of vacuum)
                \: Every factorial vacuum representation is irreducible. A vacuum representation is irreducible iff it has an unique vacuum vector.
            %li
              %p
                %strong>
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Reeh-Schlieder+Theorem"} Reeh-Schlieder Theorem
                \: The vacuum vector is cyclic and separating for all local algebras.
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} R
                    %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x, y \in \R^4
            and define
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ≔
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi λ
                    %mi x
                    %mo +
                    %mi μ
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi λ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi μ
                    %mo ≧
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mi λ
                    %mo +
                    %mi μ
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [x, y] \coloneqq \{\lambda x + \mu y | \lambda, \mu \geqq 0, \lambda + \mu = 1\}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℳ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ≔
                  %msub
                    %mo ∩
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo ⊂
                      %mi 𝒪
                  %mi ℳ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒪
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathcal{M}[x, y] \coloneqq \cap_{[x, y] \subset \mathcal{O}} \mathcal{M}(\mathcal{O})
          .un_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              %strong triviality of algebras of spacelike segments
            %p
              If the segment
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mo +
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [x, x + b]
              is spacelike, i.e.
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                      %mi b
                      %mo ,
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle b, b \rangle \lt 0
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℳ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo +
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo =
                      %mi ℂ
                      %mi 𝟙
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}[x, x + b] = \mathbb{C} \mathbb{1}
          .un_theorem
            %h6#theorem_2 Theorem
            %p
              %strong triviality of algebras of points
              The conclusion of the preceding statement holds if we put
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b = 0
          %p
            %em Remark
            (physical viewpoint): The preceding theorem is sometimes summarized as
            = succeed "." do
              %em there are no non-trivial observables at the point
          %p
            The local algebras fulfill the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Borchers+property"} Borchers property
          %p There are several directions one can pursue next, for example
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                the
                %span.newWikiWord
                  classification of local algebras
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/classification+of+local+algebras"} ?
                or
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superselection+theory"} superselection theory
          %h2#references References
          %p This page is based upon:
          %ul
            %li
              Hellmut Baumgärtel: Operator algebraic Methods in Quantum Field Theory. A series of lectures. Akademie Verlag 1995 (
              %a{:href => "http://www.zentralblatt-math.org/zmath/en/advanced/?q=an:0839.46063&format=complete"}> ZMATH entry
              )
          %p A secondary reference is this:
          %ul
            %li
              Hellmut Baumgärtel, Manfred Wollenberg: Causal nets of operator algebras, Berlin: Akademie Verlag 1992 (
              %a{:href => "http://www.zentralblatt-math.org/zmath/en/advanced/?q=an:0749.46038&format=complete"}> ZMATH entry
              )
        .revisedby
